q(n,6,['prompt'],'Consider poisoning an unsigned answer in a recursive resolver through forged replies to its UDP authoritative-server queries. Which checks can make the stated off-path attack harder? Assume case-preserving servers for the case-randomisation option. Select all that apply.','Clarifyattackhop andunsigned/casecompatibility conditions.')
q(n,6,['options',0,'explanation'],'Correct. If P ports are chosen independently and uniformly, they add log2(P) bits to the guessing model. Predictability, NAT and the actual pool can reduce that benefit.','14–15bitsnotuniversallyadded.')
q(n,6,['options',1,'explanation'],'Correct under the stated compatibility assumption, when the resolver checks the echoed case. Not every authoritative server preserves question case.','Echoexactnotuniversal.')
q(n,6,['workedExample'],'A forged response must pass query matching and the resolver’s cache-admission rules. Independent random ports and checked case patterns increase the guessing space; bailiwick rules restrict admissible data. DoH only between the stub and recursive resolver does not protect this authoritative-query hop. Lower TTLs are not an authentication measure. DNSSEC provides a signed-data chain; authenticated transports provide different endpoint/hop integrity guarantees.','DNSSEConlydetectabledefencefalse.')
qr(n,7,['prompt'],'What do users behind validating resolvers see?','Assume only the new key remains, the stale DS is the sole supported authenticated DS, checking is enabled (CD=0), no local exception applies, and old validated cached data is exhausted. What happens to fresh signed answers from that zone?','Caches/CD/trustanchors/multipleDSpreventuniversalimmediateSERVFAIL.')
q(n,7,['options',3,'text'],'SERVFAIL when those answers cannot be authenticated through the stale DS','Not everynameordatafromparentnecessarilySERVFAIL.')
q(n,7,['workedExample'],'The authenticated parent DS binds the old child DNSKEY; under the assumptions, no remaining key matches it. The validator therefore classifies the fresh signed data as bogus and normally returns SERVFAIL with checking enabled. Non-validating clients or CD requests can behave differently. A real rollover must coordinate DNSKEYs, signatures and parent DS with overlap/cache timing; replacing the DS before its new key is available can also cause an outage. Follow a documented rollover method rather than treating one ordering rule as sufficient.','OriginalpublishnewDSbeforekeysunsafe simplifiedrollover.')
q(n,8,['prompt'],'A conventional static NSEC chain in example.com has adjacent names alpha.example.com and delta.example.com. Which signed interval covers the exact absent name charlie.example.com, and what information does following such a chain expose? A complete NXDOMAIN proof may additionally need wildcard denial.','Exactnamegap notfullNXDOMAINproof; onlineNSECexceptions.')
q(n,8,['options',0,'text'],'alpha.example.com NSEC delta.example.com; it exposes adjacent names in the conventional chain','Notuniversalwholezonenameenumeration.')
q(n,8,['options',0,'explanation'],'Correct. Charlie sorts strictly between alpha and delta. A validated NSEC RRset covers that gap; additional proof can be required to exclude a wildcard answer.','NSECsinglegapnotfulldenial.')
q(n,8,['options',1,'explanation'],'In the stated static chain, charlie is absent and is not an existing chain owner. The relevant signed interval is bounded by alpha and delta.','Online-signeddenialmeansuniversalnocanownerfalseoutsidemodel.')
q(n,8,['workedExample'],'In the specified canonical interval, alpha<charlie<delta. The authenticated NSEC link covers the exact-name gap, while complete denial checks also account for wildcard applicability and delegation context. Following a conventional static NSEC chain exposes its represented names. NSEC3 replaces cleartext ordering with hashed names, which still permits offline dictionary guesses; it does not promise secrecy.','Scopeenumeration/denialproperties.')
